 Month 1 Summary — Understanding, Contribution & Growth (Jarvis)
✅ What I Understood

Jarvis focuses on reducing false positive alerts in communication surveillance
High false positives increase analyst workload and reduce efficiency
The problem requires:

Semantic understanding of text
Robust preprocessing
Careful handling of class imbalance
Explainable ML for analyst trust




🛠️ What I Did

Analyzed chat‑based surveillance data and alert structure
Designed a proxy dataset to simulate production constraints
Preprocessed and standardized text data
Built end‑to‑end ML pipelines:

Feature engineering (TF‑IDF, embeddings)
Model training and evaluation


Compared multiple models and data strategies


🔍 What I Explored

Traditional ML vs embedding‑based NLP approaches
Transformer models (FinMPNet, DeBERTa)
Dimensionality reduction (UMAP)
Clustering and topic modeling (K‑Means, BERTopic)
Effects of SMOTE and feature variations on performance


⚠️ Problems Faced

No access to production Bloomberg data
High class imbalance in alerts
Ambiguous real‑world problem definitions
Model sensitivity to preprocessing and features


🧩 How I Overcame Them

Created a production‑like proxy dataset
Applied SMOTE and evaluated balanced vs unbalanced setups
Ran controlled experiments across multiple pipelines
Used explainability (SHAP) to understand model behavior
Sought guidance and iterated systematically


📈 What I Learned

Data quality drives model success more than complexity
Imbalance can silently bias predictions
Embeddings capture intent better than surface text features
No single “best” model — experimentation is essential
Interpretability is critical in surveillance systems


🌱 Month 1 Outcome

Built a strong end‑to‑end understanding of Jarvis
Transitioned from learning concepts to applied ML problem‑solving
Gained confidence in working with real‑world, ambiguous data
